WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.685
R comes with many tools, but the best ones live in packages that we add ourselves.

2
00:00:06.685 --> 00:00:11.835
In this lesson, you will learn to install packages, load them each time you

3
00:00:11.835 --> 00:00:15.881
work, and read the messages R shows you along the way.

4
00:00:15.881 --> 00:00:17.720
Let's set up our toolbox.

5
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.568
Here is our list. First, a note: installing a package.

6
00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:30.032
Under it, install dot packages, with the package name in quotes:

7
00:00:30.032 --> 00:00:35.000
tidyverse, Hmisc, corrplot, readxl, openxlsx, stargazer, dplyr, expss, and formattable.

8
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.609
You only need to install a package once on your computer.

9
00:00:44.609 --> 00:00:47.480
It is like installing an app on your phone.

10
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.762
But each time you open R, you need to load the packages you want to use.

11
00:00:56.762 --> 00:01:00.300
For that, we use library, with the package name inside.

12
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.377
I run the library lines one by one.

13
00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:11.064
The console fills with red text. Please don't panic.

14
00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:13.932
Most of it is just information, like:

15
00:01:13.932 --> 00:01:15.980
the following objects are masked.

16
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.524
Masked means two packages have a function with the same name, so R tells

17
00:01:23.524 --> 00:01:25.420
you which one it will use.

18
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.000
It is not an error.

19
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.204
One message did say error:

20
00:01:36.204 --> 00:01:38.729
there is no package called colorspace.

21
00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:41.675
That means one package needs another one.

22
00:01:41.675 --> 00:01:44.200
Installing the missing package fixes it.

23
00:01:59.100 --> 00:01:59.778
So remember:

24
00:01:59.778 --> 00:02:02.150
red text is not always an error.

25
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.200
Read it calmly, and look for the word Error.

26
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.537
Now, a new question:

27
00:02:28.537 --> 00:02:31.052
where does R look for our files?

28
00:02:31.052 --> 00:02:33.926
The function getwd, get working directory, tells us.

29
00:02:33.926 --> 00:02:36.800
Run it, and R shows the folder path.

30
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.720
Here I open the folder on the computer, to see the session folders where

31
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.040
our files are saved.

32
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.430
Next, setwd, set working directory, tells R which folder to use.

33
00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.760
We will do that carefully in the next lesson. Great progress!

